WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.960
Good to have you on Planetary and Daily episode 105, it's June 29th, 2026.

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:11.700
Let's get into today's research and health developments.

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:18.620
Plasma protein patterns now flag organ-specific risks for dementia, heart disease, and several

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:20.060
cancers years ahead.

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:26.720
Researchers analyzed roughly 7,000 proteins in plasma samples to build organ-specific

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:27.960
proteomic clocks.

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:35.720
The clocks tracked risk for all cause mortality, cardiovascular events, dementia, and multiple cancers.

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:42.460
Lifestyle factors such as physical inactivity, smoking, and heavy alcohol use shifted

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:49.340
the clocks in measurable ways. This work replicates earlier proteomic aging signatures with an

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:56.240
independent platform. A few episodes back the show examined aging biomarkers. Today's study adds

00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:01.680
organ-specific resolution that clarifies which tissues drive individual risk trajectories.

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:08.260
From molecular signatures of aging to practical interventions that can move those same risk trajectories,

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:15.600
we turn to exercise. A six-month randomized trial of more than 120 participants in their 70s

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:20.860
compared high-intensity interval training with moderate and low-intensity exercise.

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:25.960
Only the high-intensity group lost fat mass while maintaining lean muscle.

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:32.220
Moderate and low-intensity programs produce smaller fat losses accompanied by muscle decline.

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:39.400
The differential effect highlights intensity as a variable that protects sarcopenia risk during weight

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:46.280
reduction. The results sharpened questions about intensity thresholds that protect against sarcopenia

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:50.940
in older adults. While intensity protects physical resilience,

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:57.820
another preserved capacity, language processing, remains active even when consciousness is absent.

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:04.880
Patients under general anesthesia process nouns verbs and adjectives while hearing stories

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:11.680
with neural signals showing anticipation of upcoming words. Researchers recorded activity from

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:17.800
implanted electrodes during routine surgical anesthesia. The findings indicate that language

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:23.880
networks remain active even when consciousness is suppressed. The data challenged the assumption

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:30.160
that unconscious states eliminate complex linguistic computation. Most people pictured general

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:36.440
anesthesia as a complete shutdown of brain activity. In reality, the brain continues sophisticated

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:43.080
processing even while the person remains unresponsive. Right now, as you listen, your own language

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:48.840
networks are predicting the next word in this sentence. Similar predictive signals appear in

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:55.680
anesthetized patients hearing stories. The practical takeaway is that unconscious states are not uniform

00:02:55.680 --> 00:03:02.320
blanks. Future brain computer interfaces may exploit these preserved pathways for communication when

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:10.360
patients cannot move or speak. Now, shifting to a very different area of research. A modified large

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:18.400
serine recombinase now directs precise integration of sizeable DNA payloads at chosen genomic sites.

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:26.180
The retargeting method avoids random insertion and reduces off-target events. Tests in human

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:34.120
cells demonstrated one step placement of multi-kilobase sequences. The tool expands options for stable gene

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:41.540
addition beyond CRISPR cut and paste approaches. Precision genome tools often rely on accurate protein

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:48.220
structure predictions. A new method now improves those predictions for flexible proteins. Alpha-fold

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:55.900
3 typically outputs one dominant confirmation even when proteins sample multiple states. Researchers at

00:03:55.900 --> 00:04:02.200
the Institute of Science and Technology Austria combine the model with experimental data to generate

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:08.460
ensembles that match measured conditions. The method corrects for local structural changes

00:04:08.460 --> 00:04:14.460
that depend on environment or ligands. It improves accuracy for flexible regions without

00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:20.580
retraining the underlying network. Follow-up work will test whether the same guidance improves

00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:27.780
predictions for membrane proteins and large complexes. While structural tools advance, immune cell

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:34.080
lipid handling offers a different window into disease progression. Brain tissue from patients with

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:40.940
aggressive multiple sclerosis contained abundant foamy macrophages filled with fat droplets after

00:04:40.940 --> 00:04:48.080
they engulf damaged myelin. These lipid overloaded cells switch from repair functions to sustaining

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:54.340
inflammation. The observation links myelin debris clearance failure to faster disease worsening.

00:04:55.140 --> 00:05:00.520
Researchers are now examining whether clearing the lipid load can restore protective activity

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:07.100
from cellular mechanisms inside the body to mechanisms that shape entire ecosystems outside it.

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:12.740
Grasslands covering 40% of land surface were studied to test how the sequence of

00:05:12.740 --> 00:05:19.200
extinction affects ecosystem resilience. Removing certain species first reduced resistance to drought

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:25.300
and nutrient shifts more than random loss. The work shows that functional traits of the first

00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:31.940
species to disappear determine how much productivity and carbon storage remain. Models that ignore

00:05:31.940 --> 00:05:37.220
loss order therefore underestimate future risks to food production and biodiversity.

00:05:37.220 --> 00:05:45.160
Two-year trial findings add another data point on supplements and brain health. Fish oil capsules

00:05:45.160 --> 00:05:51.640
raised brain omega-3 levels yet produced no measurable change in memory, cognition, or Alzheimer's

00:05:51.640 --> 00:05:58.360
related imaging markers. The study followed participants for two years and directly compared supplemented

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:04.980
and control groups. Results shift focus from isolated supplements toward broader dietary patterns

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:11.880
that deliver the same fatty acids. The null finding aligns with earlier trials that also fail to show

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:19.020
prevention effects in cognitively healthy adults. Beneficial microbes stimulated lignin production

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:25.740
in plant roots rather than blocking salt uptake. Greenhouse and field trials showed improved growth

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:33.040
and yield on saline soils after bacterial inoculation. The mechanism gives crops a structural buffer

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:39.720
against osmotic stress. The approach offers a biological alternative to chemical amendments for

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:46.600
reclaiming salt-affected farmland. Long-term exposure data strengthen links between a common pesticide

00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:53.960
and neurodegenerative risk. UCLA researchers linked residential proximity to chlorprivose with more

00:06:53.960 --> 00:07:00.080
than twice the likelihood of developing Parkinson's disease. Laboratory experiments showed the compound

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:07.320
damages dopamine neurons and impairs clearance of toxic proteins. The population level association

00:07:07.320 --> 00:07:14.700
strengthens the case for examining pesticide residues in Parkinson's epidemiology. A living relative

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:21.380
of the earliest land plants revealed a dynamic sunscreen mechanism that adjusts to ultraviolet

00:07:21.380 --> 00:07:29.100
exposure. The system likely protected the first terrestrial flora more than 500 million years ago.

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:36.220
The study traces how this biochemical toolkit was retained and modified in modern plant lineages.

00:07:36.860 --> 00:07:42.900
University of New Hampshire analysis of solar events showed measurable drops in rain and snow

00:07:42.900 --> 00:07:48.380
in the hours and days afterward. Stronger storms produced larger reductions.

00:07:49.140 --> 00:07:55.000
The link operates through atmospheric ionization changes that alter cloud microphysics.

00:07:55.720 --> 00:08:00.920
The pattern offers a new variable for short-term weather and climate modeling.

00:08:01.300 --> 00:08:08.060
The James Webb Space Telescope identified water vapor, methane, carbon dioxide, ammonia,

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:14.260
and previously unconfirmed salt clouds in the atmosphere of a world 57 light years away.

00:08:14.840 --> 00:08:21.800
The salt aerosols help explain the planet's persistent pink hue. Spectral data now allow direct

00:08:21.800 --> 00:08:27.660
comparison of cloud chemistry across the growing sample of directly characterized exoplanets.

00:08:28.060 --> 00:08:35.620
An associated set of fossil vertebrae from Ototus Megalodon was rediscovered after missing since the

00:08:35.620 --> 00:08:43.340
1980s. Danish and international researchers re-examined the specimen. Earlier size estimates reaching

00:08:43.340 --> 00:08:49.320
approximately 80 feet received support from the vertebrae measurements. Bone proportions also

00:08:49.320 --> 00:08:56.660
suggest lifestyle details about swimming efficiency and prey size. The find closes a long-standing gap

00:08:56.660 --> 00:09:03.260
in the Megalodon fossil record. Two intentionally mummified dogs from southern Peru date to roughly

00:09:03.260 --> 00:09:11.260
1,100 years ago. One young animal was placed on a woven mat in a small pit positioned as a sleeping.

00:09:11.940 --> 00:09:17.160
The burials predate the later use of dogs as status symbols in Indian societies.

00:09:18.040 --> 00:09:23.100
The erud environment preserves soft tissue and wrapping materials that reveal deliberate

00:09:23.100 --> 00:09:28.780
funerary treatment. The finds expand the timeline for companion animal relationships,

00:09:29.380 --> 00:09:35.000
in pre-Inka cultures. Next time, keep an eye on follow-up work testing whether guidance from

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:41.540
experimental data improves alpha-fold predictions from membrane proteins. That covers today's science

00:09:41.540 --> 00:09:45.960
and health news. Share this with someone who's curious about the latest research.

00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:50.960
I'm Patrick and Vancouver. See you next time, and if you'd rather watch than listen,

